Congressional elections, midterm elections & control\par
11 | P a g e\par
Congressional elections determine who represents your state in the U.S. House \par
of Representatives and Senate. They also decide which political party will hold a \par
majority in each chamber of Congress for the next two years.\par
How Congressional elections work\par
Congressional elections happen every two years. Voters choose one-third of \par
senators and every member of the House of Representatives. Midterm elections \par
take place halfway between presidential elections.\par
U.S. House of Representatives\par
Members of the U.S. House of \par
Representatives serve two-year \par
terms. All 435 members get \par
elected every midterm and \par
presidential election year.\par
A representative must be at least \par
25, a U.S. citizen for at least \par
seven years, and live in the state \par
he or she represents. The \par
number of representatives a \par
state has depends on its \par
population. Each representative \par
serves a specific congressional \par
district.\par
U.S. Senate\par
Senators serve six-year terms. One-third of senators get elected during each \par
midterm and each presidential election year.\par
A senator must be at least 30, a U.S. citizen for at least nine years, and live in the \par
state he or she represents. There are 100 U.S. senators, two from each state.\par
State and local elections\par
State and local elections can take place in any year at various times throughout \par
the year. There can be state-wide elections for governor or state legislature.\par
A city may elect its mayor. There may be elections for judges and local officials. \par
Ballot initiatives may be up for a vote.\par
