WHAT IS THE AMERICAN DREAM?\par
Origins:\par
\lquote We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all men are created equal, \par
that they are endowed by their Creator with certain unalienable Rights, \par
that among these are Life, Liberty, and the pursuit of Happiness.\rquote\par
The United States Declaration of Independence (1776)\par
\lquote But there has been also the American dream, that dream of a land in which life \par
should be better and richer and fuller for every man, with opportunity for each \par
according to his ability or achievement. It is a difficult dream for the European upper \par
classes to interpret adequately, and too many of us ourselves have grown weary and \par
mistrustful of it. It is not a dream of motor cars and high wages merely, but a dream\par
of social order in which each man and each woman shall be able to attain to the \par
fullest stature of which they are innately capable, and be recognized by others for \par
what they are, regardless of the fortuitous circumstances of birth or position... The \par
American dream, that has lured tens of millions of all nations to our shores in the \par
past century has not been a dream of merely material plenty, though that has \par
doubtlessly counted heavily. It has been much more than that. It has been a dream \par
of being able to grow to fullest development as man and woman, unhampered by the \par
barriers which had slowly been erected in the older civilizations, unrepressed by \par
social orders which had developed for the benefit of classes rather than for the \par
simple human being of any and every class.\rquote\par
James Truslow Adams, in Epic of America (1931), coined the phrase \lquote American dream\rquote\par
