Truman\rquote s fight for national health care\par
 (From PBS)\par
In 1965 President Lyndon Baines Johnson formally signed \par
Medicare and Medicaid into law in Independence, Missouri, as \par
former president Harry S. Truman and his steadfast wife, Bess, \par
looked on with pride. As LBJ handed \ldblquote Give \lquote Em Hell Harry\rdblquote  and \par
Bess the pens he used to affix his signature to the document, the \par
President proclaimed Mr. Truman as \ldblquote the real daddy of Medicare.\rdblquote\par
Back in 1945 \f1\emdash  a mere seven months into a presidency he \par
inherited from Franklin D. Roosevelt \emdash  Truman proposed a \par
\ldblquote universal\rdblquote  national health insurance program. In his remarks to \par
Congress, he declared, \ldblquote Millions of our citizens do not now have a \par
full measure of opportunity to achieve and enjoy good health. Millions do not now have protection or \par
security against the economic effects of sickness. The time has arrived for action to help them attain \par
that opportunity and that protection.\rdblquote\par
President Truman outlined five critical goals of national health. The first was to address the \par
number and disparity of physicians, nurses and other health professionals, especially in low-income \par
and rural communities where there were \ldblquote no adequate facilities for the practice of medicine\rdblquote  and \ldblquote the \par
earning capacity of the people in some communities makes it difficult if not impossible for doctors who \par
practice there to make a living.\rdblquote  To begin to correct this problem, Truman wanted the federal \par
government to construct modern, quality hospital across the nation\emdash especially where they did not yet \par
exist.\par
The second issue was the need to develop and bolster public health services (both to control \par
the spread of infectious diseases and improve sanitary conditions across the nation) and maternal \par
and child health care. With respect to the latter, Harry Truman reminded Congress, \ldblquote the health of \par
American children, like their education, should be recognized as a definite public responsibility.\rdblquote\par
Third, he sought to increase the nation\rquote s investment in both medical research and medical \par
education.\par
The fourth problem addressed the high cost of individual medical care. \ldblquote The principal reason \par
why people do not receive the care they need,\rdblquote  Truman noted, \ldblquote is that they cannot afford to pay for it \par
on an individual basis at the time they need it. This is true not only for needy persons. It is also true \par
for a large proportion of normally self-supporting persons.\rdblquote\par
And fifth, he focused on the lost earnings that inevitably occur when serious illness strikes. \par
\ldblquote Sickness,\rdblquote  Truman cogently explained, \ldblquote not only brings doctor bills; it also cuts off income.\par
Not surprisingly, it was President Truman\rquote s proposal to fix the 4th and 5th problems with a national \par
health insurance plan that provoked the loudest opposition. Truman proposed that every wage \par
33 | P a g e\par
earning American pay monthly fees or taxes to cover the cost of all medical expenses in time of \par
illness. The plan also called for a cash balance to be paid to policyholders, in the event of injury or \par
illness, to replace the income those individuals lost.\par
His measured and careful description of the plan merits quoting:\par
\ldblquote Under the plan I suggest, our people would continue to get medical and hospital services just as they do\par
now \emdash  on the basis of their own voluntary decisions and choices. Our doctors and hospitals would\par
continue to deal with disease with the same professional freedom as now. There would, however, be this\par
all-important difference: whether or not patients get the services they need would not depend on how\par
much they can afford to pay at the time\'85None of this is really new. The American people are the most\par
insurance-minded people in the world. They will not be frightened off from health insurance because\par
some people have misnamed it \lquote socialized medicine.\rquote  I repeat \emdash  what I am recommending is not\par
socialized medicine. Socialized medicine means that all doctors work as employees of government. The\par
American people want no such system. No such system is here proposed.\rdblquote\par
The Truman plan was quickly converted into a Social Security expansion bill sponsored by \par
Sens. Robert Wagner (D-NY) and James Murray (D-MT) and Rep. John Dingell Sr. (D-MI). A version \par
of this bill had been proposed in 1943, when FDR was still president, but died in committee both \par
because of the pressures of the war and the lack of presidential pressure on Congress.\par
At first, things looked somewhat rosy for the reinvigorated 1945 bill: the Democrats still \par
controlled both the House of Representatives and the Senate and a number of prominent Americans \par
vociferously supported it. Still, the nation was weary from war, the high taxes necessary to pay for \par
FDR\rquote s New Deal, and what many Americans perceived to be a too intrusive federal government.\par
Almost as soon as the reinvigorated bill was announced, the once-powerful American Medical \par
Association (AMA) capitalized on the nation\rquote s paranoia over the threat of Communism and, despite \par
Truman\rquote s assertions to the contrary, attacked the bill as \ldblquote socialized medicine.\rdblquote  Even more outrageous, \par
the AMA derided the Truman administration as \ldblquote followers of the Moscow party line.\rdblquote  During \par
congressional hearings in 1946, the AMA proposed its own plan emphasizing private insurance \par
options, which actually represented a political shift from its previous position opposing any third party \par
members in the delivery of health care.\par
Another historical actor entering the fray was Senator Robert Taft (R-OH), who introduced the \par
Taft-Smith-Ball bill, which called for matching grants to states to subsidize private health insurance for \par
the needy. Although the AMA supported this bill, Truman was against it because he believed it would \par
halt the political progress he had made in guaranteeing every American health insurance.\par
34 | P a g e\par
Hearings and politics continued through 1946 but little progress was made. During the midterm \par
elections of 1946, the Republicans regained control of both the Senate and the House for the first \par
time since 1929, making the bill a dead issue.\par
Harry Truman continued to make health insurance a major issue of his campaign platform in \par
1948 and specifically castigated the AMA for calling his plan \ldblquote un-American\rdblquote :\par
\ldblquote I put it to you, it is un-American to visit the sick, aid the afflicted or comfort the dying? \par
I thought that was simple Christianity.\rdblquote\par
Truman famously fooled the pollsters by winning re-election in 1948 and even the Congress \par
was restored to Democratic control that fall. But this political power was no match for the AMA\rquote s \par
redoubled lobbying and advertising efforts, which were endorsed by more than 1,800 national \par
organizations, including the American Bar Association, the American Legion and the American Farm \par
Bureau Federation. Public support waned \emdash  and the bill quietly died (again) \emdash  as the middle class \par
purchased private health insurance plans, labour unions began collectively bargaining for their \par
members\rquote  health benefits, and the advent of the Korean War.\par
Truman later called the failure to pass a national health insurance program one of the most \par
bitter and troubling disappointments in his presidency. He must have been overjoyed in 1965 to watch \par
Lyndon Johnson enact a health insurance plan for the elderly and the needy. Nevertheless, the nation \par
would have to wait another 45 years before the passage of the Patient Protection and Affordable Care \par
Act of 2010, a law that remains in jeopardy after Nov. 7 [2014], when the U.S. Supreme Court took on\par
still another legal challenge to its constitutionality. That said, many would insist there remains a great \par
more work to do to make health care affordable and accessible for all Americans.\par
