World War I ended just before Eisenhower was \par
scheduled to go to Europe, frustrating the young \par
officer, but he soon managed to gain an appointment \par
to the Command and General Staff College at Fort \par
Leavenworth, Kansas. Graduating first in his class of \par
245, he served as a military aide to General John J.\par
Pershing, commander of U.S. forces during World War \par
I, and later to General Douglas MacArthur, U.S. Army \par
chief of staff. During his seven years serving under MacArthur, Eisenhower was \par
stationed in the Philippines from 1935 to 1939.\par
Eisenhower returned soon after Nazi Germany\rquote s invasion of Poland sparked the \par
outbreak of World War II in Europe. In September 1941, he received his first \par
general\rquote s star with a promotion to brigadier general. After Japan attacked Pearl \par
Harbour that December, U.S. Army Chief of Staff General George C. Marshall called \par
Eisenhower to Washington, D.C. to work as a planning officer. Beginning in \par
November 1942, Eisenhower headed Operation Torch, the successful Allied invasion \par
of North Africa. He then directed the amphibious invasion of Sicily and the Italian \par
mainland in 1943 that led to the fall of Rome in June 1944.\par
Made a full general in early 1943, Eisenhower was appointed supreme commander \par
of the Allied Expeditionary Force in December of that year and given the \par
responsibility of spearheading the planned Allied invasion of Nazi-occupied Europe. \par
On D-Day (June 6, 1944), more than 150,000 Allied forces crossed the English \par
Channel and stormed the beaches of Normandy; the invasion led to the liberation of \par
Paris on August 25 and turned the tide of the war in Europe decisively in the Allied \par
direction. Having risen from lieutenant colonel in the Philippines to supreme \par
commander of the victorious forces in Europe in only five years, Eisenhower \par
returned home to a hero\rquote s welcome in 1945 to serve as chief of staff of the U.S. \par
Army.\par
