CIVIL RIGHTS\par
The NAACP\par
1909 A group of African-American and white men and women establish the NAACP. \par
Founders include W.E.B. Du Bois (1868\f1\endash 1963). The organization is originally called \par
the National Negro Committee.\par
1930s It begins providing moral, economic, and legal support to African-Americans \par
suffering criminal injustice. In 1931, it offers legal representation to the Scottsboro \par
Boys, nine teenagers who are falsely accused of raping two white women. \par
1948 President Truman becomes the first president to formally address the NAACP. \par
He works with it to develop a commission to study and offer ideas to improve civil \par
rights in the United States. That same year, Truman signs Executive Order 9981, \par
which desegregates the United States Armed Services. \par
1950 The NAACP wins three Supreme Court victories against segregation at \par
universities and on railroad dining cars.\par
1954 The landmark Supreme Court decision, Brown v. Board of Education of \par
Topeka, overturns Plessy v. Ferguson (1896). The new decision states that racial \par
segregation violates the Equal Protection Clause of the 14th Amendment. The ruling \par
makes it unconstitutional to separate students of different races in public schools. \par
Ten years later, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 makes it illegal to racially segregate \par
public facilities.\par
1955 The NAACP seeks to establish a timescale for school desegregation, resulting \par
in the so-called Brown II Supreme Court ruling which states that schools must be \par
desegregated \lquote with all deliberate speed\rquote .\par
1955 A local chapter secretary of the NAACP, Rosa Parks, refuses to give up her \par
seat on a segregated bus in Montgomery, Alabama. Her actions set the stage for the \par
Montgomery Bus Boycott. \par
1957 After Brown, the NAACP attempts to register black students in all-white \par
schools. By 1957, it has registered nine students to attend Little Rock Central High in \par
59 | P a g e\par
Arkansas. President Eisenhower is forced to deploy the army to protect the students \par
in the face of resistance by Governor Faubus and local mobs.\par
1963 The NAACP helps to organize the March on Washington, one of the biggest \par
civil rights rallies in U.S. history.\par
1964\endash 1965 The NAACP plays a pivotal role in the passage of the Civil Rights Act of \par
1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Through cases fought and won in the U.S. \par
Supreme Court as well as grassroots initiatives such as the Freedom Summer, the \par
NAACP appeals to various levels of government to change American society.\par
The organisation received some criticism for its strategy \par
of working through the judicial system and lawmakers, \par
rather than focusing on more direct methods of protest \par
favoured by other national civil rights groups.\par
At the same time, NAACP members were subject to \par
harassment and violence. In 1963, Medgar Evers\par
(right), the first NAACP field secretary in Mississippi, was assassinated outside his \par
home in Jackson by a member of the White Citizens\rquote  Council. This group was formed \par
in 1954 in Mississippi to resist the integration of schools and civil rights activism.\par
