Evaluating the Success of the Great Society\par
2014 article\par
Civil Rights\par
On July 2, 1964, President Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act of 1964.\par
\ldblquote We believe that all men are created equal,\rdblquote  Johnson said in an address to the \par
country. \ldblquote Yet many are denied equal treatment.\rdblquote\par
The law outlawed discrimination based on race, color, national origin, religion or sex. \par
It also authorized the attorney general to bring lawsuits against schools practicing \par
segregation and discouraged job discrimination through the creation of the Equal \par
Employment Opportunity Commission. In fiscal 2013, there were 93,727 charges \par
filed with the agency; 35.3 percent involved race and 29.5 percent were based on \par
alleged sex discrimination.\par
Johnson later added to those protections with the landmark Voting Rights Act of \par
1965, parts of which were rescinded by the Supreme Court in 2013, and the Civil \par
Rights Act of 1968, which sought to eliminate discrimination in housing.\par
Johnson did away with literacy tests some Southern states required voters to take; \par
black voter registration rates in those states increased an average of 67 percent from \par
1964 to 1968. In 1970, there were 1,469 black elected officials in the United States; \par
by November 2011, there were more than 10,500.\par
Critics, noting how much progress has been made on racial equality, argue that \par
some aspects of the civil rights laws are no longer needed. In 2013, the U.S. \par
Supreme Court eliminated some parts of the Voting Rights Act, and earlier this \par
month, it upheld the state of Michigan\rquote s move to ban affirmative action.\par
War on Poverty\par
On Aug. 20, 1964, Johnson \par
signed the Economic \par
Opportunity Act, the foundation \par
of the War on Poverty. It \par
established the Office of \par
Economic Opportunity to direct \par
and coordinate educational, \par
employment and training \par
programs that laid its \par
groundwork.\par
83 | P a g e\par
Between 1965 and 1968, spending to help the poor doubled; within 10 years, the \par
percentage of Americans living below the poverty line declined to 12 percent from 20 \par
percent. The rate has fluctuated greatly in the past 50 years. According to the \par
census, 15.9 percent of Americans lived in poverty in 2012, which is just a couple of \par
points lower than where the Census estimates it stood in 1965.\par
But the president\rquote s Council of Economic Advisers uses a broader measure \emdash\par
including tax credits and benefits such as food assistance \emdash  that estimates that \par
poverty has dropped by more than a third, from more than 25 percent of the \par
population in the mid-1960s to 16 percent in 2012.\par
\ldblquote Today for the first time in all the history of the human race, a great nation is able to \par
make and is willing to make a commitment to eradicate poverty among its people,\rdblquote  \par
Johnson said in the Rose Garden.\par
Among other things it spawned was the Appalachian Regional Development Act of \par
1965, which was designed to help pull Appalachia, where one-third of residents lived \par
below the poverty line, out of hardship, develop its industries and provide \par
educational and health-care opportunities to its residents. Today, the Appalachian \par
Regional Commission is a federal-state partnership that helps fund a number of \par
projects in the region in areas including energy, infrastructure, highways and \par
telecommunications.\par
The Office of Economic Opportunity, which ran the War on Poverty, was abolished in \par
1981.\par
Education\par
On April 11, 1965, Johnson signed the Elementary and Secondary Education Act in \par
front of Junction School in Johnson City, Tex., which he attended. The act committed \par
the federal government to help, for the first time, local school districts whose \par
students come from low-income families.\par
\ldblquote As a son of a tenant farmer, I know that education is the only valid passport from \par
poverty,\rdblquote  Johnson, who \par
taught at a Hispanic \par
school in an impoverished \par
town before launching his \par
political career, said at the \par
signing. \ldblquote As a former \par
teacher \endash  and, I hope, a \par
future one \endash  I have great \par
expectations of what this \par
law will mean for all of our \par
young people. As \par
president of the United \par
States, I believe deeply Folk singer Tom Glazer performs in July 1965 for nearly 400 children \par
enrolled in Head Start centers at Saratoga Square Park in Brooklyn, N.Y. \par
84 | P a g e\par
no law I have signed or will ever sign means more to the future of America.\rdblquote\par
A month later, Head Start launched, a program designed to give underprivileged \par
children a \ldblquote head start\rdblquote  before starting first grade. Lady Bird Johnson served as \par
honorary chair of the program. Head Start has served more than 31 million children \par
from birth to age 5 since 1965. In 2012-13, 1.13 million children and pregnant \par
women were served by Head Start, according to the program. The vast majority \endash  82 \par
percent \endash  were children ages 3 and 4.\par
Later in 1965, Johnson launched the Higher Education Act, meant to open up college \par
to anyone who wanted to attend through scholarships and low-income loans. It also \par
established a national teacher corps.\par
In the 1963-64 school year, $879 million in federal grants were given to students, \par
almost all to veterans or members of the military, and $849 million was doled out in \par
student loans. In 2012-13, students received $185.1 billion in aid; federal loans \par
constituted 37 percent of that total and federal grants 24 percent, according to the \par
College Board.\par
Health\par
Johnson signed an amendment to the Social Security Act creating Medicaid and \par
Medicare, health insurance programs for the elderly and low-income individuals and \par
families, in a ceremony at the Harry S. Truman Library in Independence, Mo., on July \par
30, 1965.\par
\ldblquote No longer will older Americans be denied the healing miracle of modern medicine. \par
No longer will illness crush and destroy the savings that they have so carefully put \par
away over a lifetime so that they might enjoy dignity in their later years. No longer \par
will young families see their own incomes, and their own hopes, eaten away simply \par
because they are carrying out their deep moral obligations to their parents, and to \par
their uncles, and their aunts,\rdblquote  Johnson said. \ldblquote And no longer will this nation refuse the \par
hand of justice to those who have given a lifetime of service and wisdom and labor to \par
the progress of this progressive country.\rdblquote\par
In 1966 about 19 million people \endash  all of them elderly \endash  were enrolled in Medicare. By \par
2010, just under 47 million people \endash  both elderly and disabled \endash  participated in the \par
program. In 1975, about 22 million people were served by Medicaid; currently, 62 \par
million people participate in the program.\par
