Arts & Media\par
On Nov. 7, 1967, Johnson \par
signed the Public \par
Broadcasting Act, providing \par
financial assistance for non\'02commercial television and \par
radio broadcasting, including \par
PBS and NPR. Have you \par
watched \ldblquote Sesame Street\rdblquote  or\par
\ldblquote Mr. Rogers\rquote  Neighborhood\rdblquote ? \par
Thank the Great Society.\par
Today there are 987 stations \par
nationwide \endash  most locally \par
owned and operated \endash  that \par
broadcast NPR programming.\par
The Great Society also led to the fruition of the John F. Kennedy Center for the \par
Performing Arts in Washington and created the National Endowment for the \par
Humanities, which is one of the largest arts and culture funders in the United States.\par
Environment\par
On Nov. 21, 1967, \par
Johnson signed the Air \par
Quality Act, which granted \par
the government increased \par
authority to control air \par
pollution.\par
\ldblquote Don\rquote t we really risk our \par
own damnation every day \par
by destroying the air that \par
gives us life?\rdblquote  Johnson \par
asked in the East Room \par
of the White House. \ldblquote I \par
think we do. We have \par
done it with our science, our industry, and our progress. Above all, we have really \par
done it with our own \par
carelessness \endash  our own \par
continued indifference and our own repeated negligence.\rdblquote\par
Johnson\rquote s action made many realize that clean air was vital. But the act designated \par
a regional approach to combating air pollution, something many thought would be \par
difficult to enforce. So in 1970, Congress passed the Clean Air Act, which laid out \par
nationwide standards for pollution control. According to the EPA, cars are 99 percent \par
cleaner for common pollutants now than in 1970.\par
The still-under-construction City Hall in Boston shrouded in smog in November 1966.\par
New York Jets quarterback Joe Namath appears on the public \par
television show \ldblquote Sesame Street\rdblquote  in September 1972.\par
86 | P a g e\par
Johnson also pushed through the Water Quality Act, which required states to \par
establish and enforce water quality standards for interstate waterways, and the \par
Endangered Species Act, which provided threatened animals with limited protection \par
for the first time. Currently there are 1,190 species of plants and animals on the U.S. \par
endangered species list.\par
He also signed the Wilderness Act, which preserved 9.2 million acres as federal \par
wilderness areas; the Wild and Scenic Rivers Act, which established a national \par
system to protect and preserve rivers; and the National Trails System Act, which \par
created a nationwide system of scenic and recreational trails.\par
Housing and Urban Development\par
The Omnibus Housing Bill of 1965 was a $7.5 billion measure that Johnson called \par
\ldblquote the single most important breakthrough\rdblquote  in housing in decades. \ldblquote And in the years to \par
come I believe this act will become known as the single most important housing \par
legislation in our history,\rdblquote  Johnson said at a Rose Garden signing ceremony on Aug. \par
10, 1965. The bill provided hefty rent subsidies for low-income people who moved \par
into new housing projects, created grants to help low-income homeowners \par
rehabilitate their properties, aided small businesses displaced by urban renewal and \par
grants to rehabilitate blighted urban property. Johnson signed the Housing and \par
Urban Development Act in 1965, which created the U.S. Department of Housing and \par
Urban Development, a Cabinet-level agency.\par
The Demonstration Cities and Metropolitan Development Act of 1966 gave federal \par
money to rebuild and revitalize poor, blighted urban areas. \ldblquote The Congress hereby \par
finds and declares that improving the quality of urban life is the most critical domestic \par
A modern high-rise apartment building, part of an urban renewal project, contrasts with a nearby tenement \par
building in Chicago in November 1963.\par
87 | P a g e\par
problem facing the United States,\rdblquote  the law read. It led to the creation of \ldblquote city \par
demonstration agencies,\rdblquote  or planning agencies to review development proposals that \par
now exist in virtually all metropolitan areas.\par
The Community Action Program created Community Action Agencies, nonprofits \par
dedicated to fighting poverty at the local level. \ldblquote Through a new Community Action \par
program, we intend to strike at poverty at its source \endash  in the streets of our cities and \par
on the farms of our countryside among the very young and the impoverished old. \par
This program asks men and women throughout the country to prepare long-range \par
plans for the attack on poverty in their own local communities,\rdblquote  Johnson told \par
Congress on March 16, 1964. Today there are about 1,100 Community Action \par
Agencies nationwide. According to their national membership organization the \par
agencies help 17 million people each year.\par
Not all programs were successes. The Model Cities program, for instance, was shut \par
down in 1974 after largely failing in its efforts at urban renewal.\par
Consumer Protection\par
The Great Society produced a number of laws to protect consumers, including truth\'02in-packaging requirements which Johnson said will \ldblquote will mean that the American \par
family will get full and fair value for every penny, dime and dollar that that family \par
spends,\rdblquote  at a bill signing in November 1966. Also part of his suite of bills on \par
consumer protection were the truth-in-lending for borrowers and meat and poultry \par
laws to enhance food safety. It created the Consumer Product Safety Commission \par
and the Child Safety Act to ensure that toys, medicine bottles and other products \par
were safe for both children and adults.\par
\ldblquote It will ban the sale or use of toys and other children\rquote s articles that contain dangerous \par
or deadly substances. It will ban the sale of other household articles so hazardous \par
that even labels cannot make them safe. Now there is a law that says the eyes of a \par
doll will not be poisonous beans. Now there is a law that says what looks like candy \par
will not be deadly firecracker balls. Now there is a law that says Johnny will not die \par
because his toy truck was painted with a poison. Both these laws offer sweeping \par
new protection to the American family,\rdblquote  Johnson said.\par
If you\rquote ve ever purchased a pack of cigarettes, you\rquote ve seen this warning: \ldblquote Caution: \par
Cigarette Smoking May Be Hazardous to Your Health\rdblquote  and have Johnson\rquote s Cigarette \par
Labeling and Advertising Act to thank.\par
Immigration\par
Standing at the foot of the Statue of Liberty, Johnson signed the Immigration Act on \par
Oct. 3, 1965. It abolished the national origins formula that had been in place since \par
1924, meaning that preference was no longer given to immigrants from some \par
European countries.\par
88 | P a g e\par
\ldblquote This system violated the basic principle of American democracy \endash  the principle that \par
values and rewards each man on the basis of his merit as a man,\rdblquote  Johnson said. \ldblquote It \par
has been un-American in the highest sense.\rdblquote\par
The law greatly increased the number of immigrants from Asia, Africa and other parts \par
of the world, though it continued to restrict the number of immigrants allowed into the \par
United States each year. In 1965, 296,697 people obtained legal permanent resident \par
status; in 2012, 1.03 million people became legal permanent residents. From 1960 to \par
1969, there were 358,563 people from Asia who became legal permanent residents. \par
In 2012 alone, 416,488 people from Asia were granted that status, according to the \par
Department of Homeland Security.\par
Refugees who fled Communist China arrive in San Francisco in March 1959. The Immigration \par
Act of 1965 would expand immigration from Asia, Africa and Latin America.\par
