The end of escalation in Vietnam\par
Johnson\rquote s TV address of 31 March 1968\par
Tonight, I have ordered our aircraft and our naval vessels to make no attacks on \par
North Vietnam, except in the area north of the demilitarized zone where the \par
continuing enemy buildup directly threatens allied forward positions and where the \par
movements of their troops and supplies are clearly related to that threat. \'85 It is to \par
save the lives of brave men\f1\emdash and to save the lives of innocent women and children. \par
It is to permit the contending forces to move closer to a political settlement.\par
And tonight, I call upon the United Kingdom and I call upon the Soviet Union\emdash as \par
cochairmen of the Geneva Conferences, and as permanent members of the United \par
Nations Security Council\emdash to do all they can to move from the unilateral act of \par
deescalation that I have just announced toward genuine peace in Southeast Asia. \'85 \par
I call upon President Ho Chi Minh to respond positively, and favorably, to this new \par
step toward peace. \'85 Peace will come because Asians were willing to work for it\emdash\par
and to sacrifice for it\emdash and to die by the thousands for it.\par
But let it never be forgotten: Peace will come also because America sent her sons to \par
help secure it. \'85 Yet, I believe that now, no less than when the decade began, this \par
generation of Americans is willing to "pay any price, bear any burden, meet any \par
hardship, support any friend, oppose any foe to assure the survival and the success \par
of liberty." Since those words were spoken by John F. Kennedy, the people of \par
America have kept that compact with mankind's noblest cause. And we shall \par
continue to keep it. \'85 What we won when all of our people united just must not now \par
be lost in suspicion, distrust, selfishness, and politics among any of our people.\par
Believing this as I do, I have concluded that I should not permit the Presidency to \par
become involved in the partisan divisions that are developing in this political year.\par
With America's sons in the fields far away, with America's future under challenge \par
right here at home, with our hopes and the world's hopes for peace in the balance \par
every day, I do not believe that I should devote an hour or a day of my time to any \par
personal partisan causes or to any duties other than the awesome duties of this \par
office\emdash the Presidency of your country.\par
Accordingly, I shall not seek, and I will not accept, the nomination of my party for \par
another term as your President.\par
