The Counterculture\par
When and what: roughly 1964-\par
1972, coinciding with the \par
Vietnam War, when the norms of \par
the 1950s were largely rejected \par
by youth, especially with respect \par
to the war, racial segregation, \par
sexual mores, women\rquote s rights, \par
traditional authority, and \par
materialism.\par
Hippies were the main\par
counterculture group, and \par
comprised mostly white members \par
of the middle class.\par
The movement was given disproportionate coverage in the media and divided the \par
country. To some, it reflected American ideals of free speech, equality, and world \par
peace; to others, it reflected a self-indulgent, unpatriotic assault on the moral \par
order. There was a loss of respect for parents and grandparents. \par
Widespread economic prosperity meant the white, middle-class youth\f1\emdash who \par
made up the bulk of the counterculture\emdash had sufficient leisure time to take part.\par
Hallmarks: unconventional appearance, music, drugs (LSD was promoted \par
by Dr Timothy Leary), communitarian experiments, and sexual liberation\par
(the pill had become widely available). \par
Ideals: peace, love, harmony, music, and mysticism. \par
Peak: 1967 Summer of Love. Up to 100,000 flocked to Haight-Ashbury (\lquote Hashbury\rquote )\par
district of San Francisco. The counterculture came into public awareness. Another \par
important area was the East Village in New York.\par
Culture: psychedelic rock music, pop-art. The Beatles, The \par
Grateful Dead, Jefferson Airplane, Jimi Hendrix, The Doors, The \par
Rolling Stones, Neil Young, Bob Dylan, Janis Joplin, Pink \par
Floyd. Andy Warhol. The musical Hair; the film Easy Rider.\par
August 15-18, 1969, the Woodstock Music Festival was held in \par
Bethel, New York, with an audience of half a million.\par
Key sayings: \ldblquote make love not war\rdblquote ; \ldblquote do your own thing\rdblquote ; \ldblquote turn \par
on, tune in, drop out\rdblquote ; \ldblquote whatever turns you on\rdblquote ; \ldblquote eight miles \par
high\rdblquote ; \ldblquote sex, drugs, and rock \lquote n\rquote  roll\rdblquote ; \ldblquote light my fire\rdblquote .\par
Evolution from Beats and beatniks in the 1950s to hippies: Allen \par
Ginsberg was a key personality of the antiwar movement.\par
