Nixon\rquote s political legacy\par
7 Fun Facts\par
1. Nixon and Franklin Roosevelt are the only two people to appear \par
on a national presidential ticket five times. Nixon won election in \par
four of the five races, as did FDR. Nixon was the winning vice\'02presidential candidate in 1952 and 1956, and he won the 1968 and \par
1972 presidential elections. Roosevelt won four presidential elections \par
and lost as a vice presidential candidate in 1920.\par
2. Nixon had a chance to attend Harvard but had to decline. As a student, Richard \par
Nixon was third in his class and was offered a tuition grant to Harvard, but he was \par
needed at home by his family.\par
3. He was also an outstanding law student. After graduating from Whittier College in \par
California, Nixon received a full scholarship to Duke Law and graduated third in his \par
class.\par
4. Nixon was a lieutenant commander in the Navy during World War II. He served in \par
a variety of administrative positions but didn\rquote t see combat during his time in the Pacific.\par
5. Richard Nixon almost became an FBI agent in the 1930s. Nixon applied to the \par
agency but never heard back about his application. He had been accepted but then \par
budget cuts eliminated his position.\par
6. Nixon was only 39 years old when he ran for vice president in 1952. Presidential \par
candidate Dwight Eisenhower didn\rquote t have a preference for a running mate, and party \par
leaders favoured Nixon for his anti-Communist stance and his strength in California.\par
7. Nixon survived two potentially fatal political losses in 1960 and 1962. Defeats in \par
the presidential campaign against John F. Kennedy and the 1962 Californian governor\rquote s \par
race led many political observers to predict Nixon\rquote s career was over. Within six years, he \par
was President after winning a hard-fought campaign in 1968. \par
