The \lquote liberal\rquote  Nixon\par
The surprisingly liberal Family Assistance Plan was voted down in the Senate, but \par
these other \lquote liberal\rquote  measures saw more success:\par
The New Economic Policy: Prior to 1973 the most important of Nixon\rquote s domestic \par
problems was the economy. To reduce inflation he tried to restrict federal spending, \par
but beginning in 1971 his budget proposals contained deficits of several billion \par
dollars, the largest in American history up to that time. The New Economic Policy, \par
announced on television in Aug 1971 in response to continuing inflation, increasing \par
unemployment, and a deteriorating trade deficit, included an 8% devaluation of the \par
dollar, new surcharges on imports, and an unprecedented peacetime freeze on \par
wages and prices for 90 days. These policies were popular and produced \par
temporary improvements by the 1972 election but, once price and wage controls \par
were lifted, inflation returned with a vengeance, reaching 8.8% in 1973 and 12.2% in \par
1974. This was exacerbated by the energy crisis: support for Israel in the Arab\'02Israeli War of 1973 led to an oil embargo on the US, damaging the economy further. \par
These policies (the \lquote Nixon Shock\rquote ) also led to the end of the Bretton Woods \par
Agreement and the convertibility of US dollars into gold. They were the catalyst for \par
the stagflation of the 1970s as the dollar devalued.\par
Social programmes: Nixon expanded existing programmes such as Social Security \par
(by 52% in his first term with future benefits indexed to the cost of living), Medicare \par
and Medicaid, and food stamps. A new Supplemental Security Income (SSI)\par
federalised existing state aid, successfully targeting vulnerable people. Poverty \par
among older Americans all but ceased.\par
Affirmative action: Nixon used an executive order to put the Philadelphia Plan \par
(1970) into effect to make government contractors hire more minority workers. \par
However, by breaking the hold of unions over construction jobs he was also helping \par
to drive down labour costs, which were contributing to inflation and business \par
concerns.\par
Women\rquote s rights: In his second press conference in Feb 1969, a reported \par
questioned why Nixon had put so few women into government positions. In \par
response, he created a Presidential Task Force on Women\rquote s Rights that year and \par
instructed the Justice Department to enforce Title VII of the Civil Rights Act. He \par
directed the heads of government agencies to develop affirmative action plans for \par
women. As a result, many more women were promoted to senior jobs in the civil \par
service and government. He also supported the Equal Rights Amendment, which \par
passed both Houses in 1972 (it would go on to fall short of ratification by three \par
states).\par
Indian rights: The 1960s had seen a powerful American Indian movement and an \par
Indian Civil Rights Act (1968). Nixon went further by laying the groundwork for the \par
Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act (1975), which empowered \par
native nations to exercise sovereignty and control their own affairs. This ended once \par
and for all the government\rquote s policy of assimilation.\par
108 | P a g e\par
The environment: Nixon established the Environmental Protection Agency in 1970, \par
which brought different agencies under one roof, and passed a major expansion of \par
the Clean Air Act in the same year, as well as other environmental legislation. He \par
supported the massive rallies and events of Earth Day in 1970 in which 20 million \par
people took part. In response to the growing environmental movement, policy shifted \par
away from new transport projects \endash  supersonic aircraft and highway extensions were \par
cancelled \endash  to mass transportation. However, once business began to protest \par
against new measures and the issue become one of growth versus pollution \par
reduction, Nixon abandoned his support for the movement.\par
