Richard Nixon: the rise \par
and fall of an American \par
president\par
In the annals of political scandals, one \par
name stands above all others: Watergate. \par
Writing in BBC History Revealed, Nige \par
Tassell reveals how this botched burglary \par
turned Richard Nixon from one-time \par
darling of American politics into one of the least-popular US presidents of the 20th \par
century\par
\ldblquote I, Richard Milhous Nixon, do solemnly swear that I will faithfully execute the office of \par
the President of the United States and will, to the best of my ability, preserve, protect \par
and defend the Constitution of the United States.\rdblquote\par
These were the words by which the 37th US President was sworn in on 20 January \par
1969. Five and a half years later, these words would ring hollow.\par
Nixon\rquote s journey to the White House had followed a somewhat more serpentine route \par
than the limousine ride along Pennsylvania Avenue that took him to his new home \par
later that day. In 1942, after five years of practising law in his native California, he \par
headed east to Washington to take employment in the federal government. There his \par
political career would both be patiently made \f1\endash  and then carelessly smashed.\par
After serving in the US Navy during World War II, Nixon sought public office for the \par
first time. He successfully ran for the House of Representatives in 1946, defeating \par
the Democratic incumbent Jerry Voorhis in the elections for California\rquote s 12th \par
congressional district.\par
His victory was, to a great extent, down to his legal training, to the way his lawyer\rquote s \par
mind worked. Nixon argued that, by supposedly being endorsed by a communist\'02connected group (a false accusation), Voorhis must be sympathetic to the \par
communist cause and harbour leftwing tendencies. Nixon\rquote s logic chimed with the \par
electorate who elected him to power. He had publicly shown himself to be staunchly \par
anti-communist, a stance that saw him swiftly co-opted onto the infamous House Un\'02American Activities Committee, as well as co-sponsoring anti-communism bills in \par
Congress.\par
112 | P a g e\par
Until this point, Nixon\rquote s profile was largely limited to the Californian district he \par
represented and to the political community in Washington. He became nationally \par
known through his dogged pursuit of Alger Hiss, an ex-employee of the State \par
Department suspected of being a \par
Soviet spy. That Hiss was convicted \par
of perjury \endash  relating to his passing of \par
government papers to a senior editor \par
at Time magazine who had \par
previously been a communist spy \endash\par
was due to Nixon\rquote s dog-with- a-bone \par
tenacity.\par
The Hiss case cemented Nixon\rquote s reputation as an unyielding enemy of communism \par
Why was Nixon called Tricky Dick?\par
Nixon\rquote s strong and very visible anti-communist outlook ensured his comfortable \par
reelection in 1948. After serving two terms as a congressman, he stood for the \par
Senate. His campaign against the Democrat candidate Helen Gahagan Douglas was \par
bitter and acrimonious, and earned him the nickname that would intermittently \par
shadow him for the rest of his political life: Tricky Dick.\par
His political acumen in his Senate campaign was, again, shown to be the product of \par
his courtroom experience. By indicating that his opponent\rquote s voting record as a \par
Congresswoman mirrored that of another Democrat popularly believed to have \par
communist sympathies, Nixon argued that she must be cut from the same cloth. He \par
walked the election.\par
Nixon only served a third of his senatorial term. This wasn\rquote t due to scandal or \par
controversy; quite the opposite. His hefty support in California (the most populous \par
state in the country and thus the state with the largest collection of Electoral College \par
votes), combined with his anti-communist rhetoric and actions, made him the ideal \par
running mate for Dwight D Eisenhower\rquote s presidential election campaign. The \par
partnership of Eisenhower\rquote s war hero and Nixon\rquote s youthful politico (he was still only \par
39) was the dream ticket. Even a modest controversy involving Nixon\rquote s campaign \par
expenses couldn\rquote t derail the duo, and they swept to power.\par
Nixon, vocal in denouncing the spread of global communism as a senator, was \par
handed an unprecedented workload for a vice-president when it came to foreign \par
policy. And when, in 1955, Eisenhower suffered a heart attack, Nixon assumed the \par
role of commander-in-chief during the President\rquote s six-week hospitalisation. Praised \par
for confidently stepping up to the plate, no-one would have had any doubt that this \par
was a man who fancied the Oval Office as his own one day.\par
113 | P a g e\par
Eisenhower and Nixon toast a second term in office in 1957\par
When did Nixon become president?\par
In 1960, four years after the Eisenhower-Nixon ticket had secured its second term, \par
the vice-president did indeed throw his hat into the ring. He was a shoo-in for the \par
Republication Party presidential nomination and faced the preferred Democrat \par
candidate, the dashing John F Kennedy, for the ultimate prize. Political historians \par
believe that one particular televised debate between the pair cost Nixon the election. \par
In the era of black-and-white television, Kennedy\rquote s dark suit made him appear more \par
commanding compared to the gaunt-looking Republican, whose mid-tone jacket \par
somewhat blended into the studio backdrop. The election result was narrow \endash  and \par
rather discoloured by claims of vote rigging in key Democrat states \endash  but Kennedy \par
took the presidency. Nixon\rquote s seemingly unstoppable march to the White House had \par
been halted in its tracks.\par
Nixon and his family retreated to California where he returned to practising law, as \par
well as writing a volume of memoirs. It looked for all the world as though his political \par
career was prematurely over. As he sat down at his typewriter, he admitted as much. \par
\ldblquote Although anyone who goes through a presidential campaign feels immediately \par
afterward that he has lived enough for a lifetime, I still did not believe I had reached \par
the point in life for memoir-writing.\rdblquote\par
Within two years of his defeat to Kennedy though, Nixon was again seeking public \par
office. But his defeat to Pat Brown for the governorship of his beloved California was \par
surely the death knell of his political ambitions. Surely.\par
114 | P a g e\par
Nixon showed himself to be a loyal Republican, lending substantial support to the \par
party during the 1964 presidential campaign of Barry Goldwater and during the mid\'02term elections two years later. He had decided not to run for the White House in \par
1964 as he viewed, in the wake of the Kennedy assassination, that the Democrats, \par
in the form of vice-president Lyndon B Johnson, would be near impossible to beat. \par
Goldwater\rquote s heavy defeat vindicated the decision.\par
By the time the next presidential campaign came around, Nixon was ready to re\'02enter the fight, the year 1968 was a tumultuous one in the US, a 12-month period \par
pockmarked by assassinations (those of both Martin Luther King Jr and Robert \par
Kennedy), demonstrations against the Vietnam War, and violence at the Democratic \par
National Convention. The country was in turmoil and Nixon took full advantage, \par
appealing to what he called \ldblquote the great silent majority\rdblquote , the moderate conservatives \par
confused and appalled by so much social unrest. He pledged to reduce crime and \par
bring an end to the war in Southeast Asia.\par
With Johnson withdrawing from the campaign for the Democratic presidential \par
nomination at an early stage, Nixon\rquote s chief opponent was the incumbent vice\'02president, Hubert Humphrey. But there was also a third candidate \endash  former Alabama \par
Governor George Wallace, standing for his American Independent Party. Wallace\rquote s \par
presence on the ballot paper split the traditional Democrat vote in the southern \par
states, helping the Republicans to return to the White House after an eight-year \par
absence. For Nixon, that limousine ride back down Pennsylvania Avenue to his new \par
home had been a long time coming.\par
Richard Nixon and the Watergate scandalNixon\rquote s departure from the White \par
House, on 9 August 1974 was not as leisurely and as triumphant as his arrival had \par
been half a decade earlier. Looked on by family, political colleagues and White \par
115 | P a g e\par
House staff, he wearily climbed the five steps of the presidential helicopter parked on \par
the south lawn. He turned to wave defiantly and \endash  a little curiously bearing in mind \par
the circumstances \endash  offer a wide smile.\par
As the helicopter rose into the sky, the President \endash  disgraced by the wrongdoings, \par
deception and illegality at the heart of his administration \endash  would have gazed down \par
on the Capitol, the scene of his two inaugurations. At the first, he had declared that \par
\ldblquote our destiny offers not the cup of despair but the chalice of opportunity\rdblquote . Yet he was \par
leaving the highest office in the land in the deepest despair, at the lowest ebb that it \par
had ever been.\par
The evening before, Nixon had made a televised address to inform the nation of his \par
intention to resign the presidency. His speech showed signs of the similar defiance \par
shown the following day on the helicopter steps, keen to linger on his \par
accomplishments in office rather than the shame under which he was departing. He \par
spoke of \ldblquote a time of achievement in which we can all be proud, achievements that \par
represent the shared efforts of the administration, the Congress and the people\rdblquote .\par
While his presidency wasn\rquote t without successes (an example being a hugely symbolic \par
visit to China), history will always view Nixon\rquote s administration as having more in the \par
debit column than the credit. Just a few months into his first term of office \endash  and \par
despite acknowledging that \ldblquote the greatest honour history can bestow is the title of \par
peacemaker\rdblquote  \endash  he authorised the secret carpet-bombing of Cambodia. But the most \par
politically damaging of his immoral practices was the scandal that dwarfed all other \par
scandals: Watergate.\par
In the presidential election of November 1972, Nixon redrew the political landscape. \par
Forty-nine states voted for him; the electoral map turned almost exclusively \par
Republican red. Only Massachusetts and the District of Columbia filed a Democrat \par
victory. But it would later transpire that this extraordinary landslide may not have \par
been secured by means completely above board. In June of that year, five men had \par
been caught breaking into the headquarters of the Democratic National Committee, \par
which were in the Watergate complex of buildings in Washington. It had all the \par
hallmarks of a political burglary: documents had been photographed and phones \par
tapped. Washington Post reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein revealed that \par
one of the burglars was on the payroll of the Committee to Re-Elect the President \par
(derisively known as CREEP). The revelations caused panic within Nixon\rquote s staff.\par
Woodward and Bernstein\rquote s source for their continuing stream of stories surrounding \par
the break-in was a reliable one: a shadowy figure named Deep Throat who was only \par
identified in 2005 to be FBI associate director Mark Felt. Felt had access to all of the \par
investigation\rquote s ongoing findings and, through the two twentysomething reporters, \par
found a channel by which he could circumnavigate any White House decrees.\par
116 | P a g e\par
Despite the Washington Post\rquote s hard-hitting headlines (\lquote FBI Finds Nixon Aides \par
Sabotaged Democrats\rquote  is just one example), the President continued to cruise \par
towards that landslide re-election, issuing a string of denials that seemed to assuage \par
any doubters among the electorate. It wasn\rquote t until spring 1973, a few months into \par
Nixon\rquote s second term, that the official investigation gathered pace. The burglars had \par
pleaded guilty at the start of their trial in January; by March, one of them \endash  a former \par
CIA operative called James McCord \endash  revealed that the burglary wasn\rquote t a CIA \par
mission, but did confirm that government officials were involved. The federal \par
investigation now focused its crosshairs on those surrounding the President.\par
The following month, White House counsel John Dean, the President\rquote s closest legal \par
advisor, began cooperating with the investigators, while still in his position \endash  and still \par
the main individual charged with keeping Nixon\rquote s name out of the whole affair. His \par
testimony was dynamite, shifting their angle away from the actual events before and \par
during the Watergate break-in and towards a conspiracy at the very pinnacle of US \par
politics. Particularly powerful was Dean\rquote s confession that he had directly discussed \par
the cover-up with the President on no fewer than 35 occasions.\par
When did Nixon resign?\par
The heat was very much being turned up. In an attempt to diffuse the mounting \par
tension, Nixon fired Dean on 30 April 1973, the same day that the resignations of two \par
other high-ranking White House advisors \endash  John Ehrlichman and Harry \lquote Bob\rquote  \par
Haldeman \endash  were announced.\par
A significant development came in July when the investigation learned that the Oval \par
Office was fitted with a secret taping system, which allowed Nixon to record all \par
117 | P a g e\par
conversations and phone calls. Special Prosecutor Archibald Cox subpoenaed the \par
recordings, to which the White House responded with transcripts, not the actual \par
tapes. A few months later, Cox was dismissed by the President, an event known as \par
the Saturday Night Massacre.\par
Nixon continued to maintain that he only had knowledge of the break-in after the \par
event. In November 1973, he held a televised press conference in which he fiercely \par
denied any wrongdoing on his part: \ldblquote People have got to know whether or not their \par
President is a crook. Well, I\rquote m not a crook.\rdblquote  The same month came the discovery that \par
more than 18 minutes of the White House tapes were blank. A secretary claimed \par
she\rquote d wiped them accidentally, but the excuse, not overwhelmed by credibility, \par
served to cast more suspicion onto Tricky Dick.\par
The legal fight over the release of the tapes continued well into 1974 until, in July, \par
the Supreme Court decreed that the full tapes, and not selected transcripts, must be \par
released. The discovery of the so-called Smoking Gun Tape \endash  in which Nixon could \par
be heard suggesting the CIA instruct the FBI to halt their investigation into the \par
Watergate break in on the grounds of national security \endash  marked the point of no \par
return for the doomed President.\par
With support from his fellow Republicans in Washington having dissolved, and the \par
realisation that he faced almost certain impeachment in Congress, Nixon fell on his \par
sword, announcing his resignation in that televised address. The helicopter\rquote s \par
departure from the White House lawn the following morning drew to an end arguably \par
the most ignominious episode in 20th-century presidential history.\par
What happened to Nixon after Watergate?\par
When Gerald Ford succeeded the departing Richard Nixon in the White House, he \par
noted \ldblquote our long national nightmare is over\rdblquote . This declaration, though, didn\rquote t stop Ford \par
fully pardoning his predecessor within the first month of his presidency, thus \par
removing any threat of Nixon being indicted.\par
As well as penning several books, in 1977 Nixon took part in a series of television \par
interviews with David Frost during which he spoke at length about Watergate. After \par
being shunned by world leaders in the immediate post-Watergate years, Nixon\rquote s \par
reputation was somewhat revived in the 1980s when he undertook a number of \par
overseas trips, including visiting Soviet premier Mikhail Gorbachev in Moscow.\par
