President Gerald Ford\rquote s pardon of Richard \par
Nixon in 1974 generated a national controversy, \par
but in recent years, some of the pardon\rquote s biggest \par
critics have changed their tunes on the \par
unprecedented move.\par
Watergate reporters Carl Bernstein and Bob \par
Woodward had vehemently opposed the pardon \par
after Ford went on national television on the same \par
day \endash  September 8, 1974 \endash  to denounce it. But in \par
recent years, the former Washington Post\par
journalists have approved of Ford\rquote s move to absolve \par
Nixon of any criminal charges related to the \par
Watergate break-in and its cover-up.\par
In a July 2014 panel hosted by the Post, Woodward \par
called the pardon \ldblquote an act of courage.\rdblquote  He had talked \par
with Ford decades after the pardon and said the \par
former President made a \ldblquote very compelling \par
argument\rdblquote  for his actions based on national security \par
and economic needs.\par
The late Senator Ted Kennedy said in 2001 that \par
while he initially opposed the pardon, he had come \par
to accept it as the best move for the country. And \par
Richard Ben-Veniste, a former Watergate \par
prosecutor and a Democrat, wrote about the pardon \par
shortly after Ford\rquote s passing in 2006.\par
\ldblquote Did Ford make the right decision in pardoning his \par
predecessor? The answer to that question is more \par
nuanced than either the howls of outrage that \par
greeted the pardon three decades ago or the \par
general acceptance with which it is viewed now,\rdblquote  \par
Ben-Veniste said in a commentary for the Post.\par
\ldblquote The decision to pardon Nixon was a political \par
judgment properly within the bounds of Ford's \par
constitutional authority,\rdblquote  he argued. \ldblquote Jerry Ford \par
acted in accord with what he sincerely felt were the \par
best interests of the country; that there was no \par
secret quid pro quo with Nixon for a pardon in return \par
for resignation; and that Ford, a compassionate \par
man, was moved by the palpable suffering of a man \par
who had lost so much.\rdblquote\par
But in the months \par
after the pardon back \par
in 1974, most \par
Americans didn\rquote t \par
approve. In a Gallup \par
poll taken in 1979, 53 \par
percent disapproved \par
of the pardon. However, in a 1986 Gallup poll, 54 \par
percent of Americans said they now approved of it.\par
Ford appeared in front of a House judiciary \par
committee in October 1974 to explain the pardon. \ldblquote I \par
was absolutely convinced then as I am now that if \par
we had had [an] indictment, a trial, a conviction, and \par
anything else that transpired after this that the \par
attention of the President, the Congress and the \par
American people would have been diverted from \par
the problems that we have to solve. And that was \par
the principle reason for my granting of the pardon,\rdblquote  \par
he said.\par
At a 2014 panel discussion, Ford\rquote s lawyer during \par
that period, Benton Becker, explained an additional \par
element that influenced Ford\rquote s decision to issue a \par
presidential pardon: a 1915 Supreme Court \par
decision. In Burdick v. United States, the Court ruled \par
that a pardon carried an "imputation of guilt" and \par
accepting a pardon was "an admission of guilt.\rdblquote\par
Thus, this decision implied that Nixon accepted his \par
guilt in the Watergate controversy by also accepting \par
Ford\rquote s pardon.\par
Prior to Ford\rquote s issuance of the pardon, Becker was \par
tasked with the difficult job of mediating the \par
negotiations between Ford and Nixon. Becker said \par
he took copies of the Burdick decision to California \par
when he met with former President Nixon, and \par
under Ford\rquote s instructions, walked through the \par
decision with Nixon.\par
After he left the White House, Ford carried part of \par
the Burdick decision with him in his wallet in case \par
someone brought up the pardon.\par
