1975: \lquote The Year of Intelligence\rquote\par
Chairman Frank Church holds up a poison dart gun as co-chairman John G. Tower looks on during a session of \par
the panel\rquote s probe of the CIA, Sept. 17, 1975\par
Watergate shocked the American public and spurred many of its representatives in \par
Congress to demand an investigation into the past activities of the FBI, CIA, NSA \par
and others. The result was the creation of two congressional committees in 1975, \par
chaired by Frank Church (D-ID) in the Senate and Otis Pike (D-NY) in the House of \par
Representatives. Their hearings exposed secret, arguably illegal wiretapping, \par
bugging, and harassment of American citizens, including Supreme Court justices, \par
reporters, and government officials, all in the name of collecting intelligence about \par
threats to national security. The most notorious case, first exposed in the 1960s and \par
fully documented by the Church Committee, was the wiretapping of Martin Luther \par
King, Jr. by the NSA and by the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover, who believed him to be \par
part of a Communist conspiracy.\par
As a result of the political pressure created by the revelations of the Church \par
Committee and the Pike Committee investigations, President Gerald Ford issued \par
