Three Mile Island Accident and Love Canal Disaster\par
Three Mile Island is the site of a nuclear \par
power plant in south central Pennsylvania. In \par
March 1979, a series of mechanical and \par
human errors at the plant caused the worst \par
commercial nuclear accident in U.S. history, \par
resulting in a partial meltdown that released \par
dangerous radioactive gasses into the \par
atmosphere. Three Mile Island stoked public \par
fears about nuclear power\f1\emdash no new nuclear \par
power plants have been built in the United States since the accident.\par
A thriller film, called The China Syndrome, hit theatres in March \par
of 1979, twelve days before the accident. The film, starring Jane \par
Fonda, Jack Lemmon and Michael Douglas, dealt with the \par
aftermath of a fictional nuclear meltdown at a reactor outside of \par
Los Angeles.\par
The nuclear industry at the time dismissed The China Syndrome\par
plot as \ldblquote sheer fiction\rdblquote . Many experts said nuclear meltdowns were \par
almost impossible. But in the early morning hours of March 28, \par
1979, a mechanical or electric failure set off an unlikely series of \par
events that led to a partial meltdown at the Unit 2 reactor. \par
Pennsylvania governor Dick Thornburgh advised pregnant women and pre-school\'02age children within a five-mile radius of the plant to evacuate the area. The crisis \par
ended three days later and there did not appear to be any significant health impact \par
on the local population.\par
Love Canal, a neighbourhood in Niagara Falls, N.Y., was the site of the worst \par
environmental disaster involving chemical wastes in U.S. history.\par
The Love Canal area was originally the site of an abandoned canal that became a \par
dumping ground for nearly 22,000 tons of chemical waste produced by the Hooker \par
Chemicals and Plastics Corporation in the 1940s and \rquote 50s. In the following years, the \par
site was filled in and given by the company to the growing city of \par
Niagara Falls, which allowed housing to be built on it. In 1978, \par
however, following serious illnesses and birth defects \endash  and \par
considerable pressure from residents \endash  officials detected the \par
leakage of toxic chemicals from underground into the basements \par
of homes in the area.\par
President Jimmy Carter declared a state of emergency in \par
August 1978 and much of Love Canal was evacuated. After \par
protracted litigation, 1,300 former residents agreed to a $20m \par
settlement of their claims. Subsequent investigations established \par
an abnormally high incidence of chromosomal damage among the area\rquote s residents.\par
