BOOK EXCERPT: The situation before Carter\rquote s \par
\lquote malaise speech\rquote  of 15 July 1979\par
July 4, Independence Day, 1979: Rain fell like \par
sheets on Washington, D.C., and suspense \par
mounted. Would the nation's biggest fireworks show \par
proceed or be drowned? National Park officials \par
huddled in rickety wood structures roped with plastic \par
and decided around 3pm to make an \par
announcement: Please be patient. Then at 5pm, \par
they announced an official delay. Four hours later, \par
they cancelled the show altogether.\par
Close-by, a different celebration got underway: The \par
annual "smoke-in" of the Youth International Party \par
(Yippies), an organization founded twelve years ago \par
by counterculture celebrities Abbie Hoffman and Jerry Rubin. There was no Vietnam \par
War to protest in 1979, of course, but the right to get high seemed as urgent as \par
stopping a war. In Lafayette Park, across from the White House, scraggly young \par
adults toked joints, swigged bottles of Jack Daniels, and set off firecrackers. The rain \par
turned the tangy smell of pot slightly mellower, but when the high wore off, the crowd \par
went berserk. The mob scrambled over a large black fence onto the White House \par
lawn. Cops pursued, dodging beer bottles flung at them. Nine arrests followed, one \par
just a few feet away from the White House.\par
Perhaps it was fortunate fewer people were on the Mall to be disappointed by \par
cancelled fireworks or freaked out by police-hippy melees. Most gas stations in D.C. \par
were shut down, not for the July 4th holiday but as a result of the decision by the \par
Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) to cut exports to the \par
United States. Stations with gas reported mile-long lines and three-hour waits. This \par
was becoming a summer when people coasted or pushed their cars to stations to \par
find no gas. Most D.C. residents simply stayed home on July 4. Those who did \par
search for gas raged. "The greatest country in the world," one person on a gas line \par
told an inquiring journalist, "is stifled by a few sheiks."\par
An editorial in the Los Angeles Times on July 4 stated the obvious: "This \par
Independence Day, the holiday's very name seems to mock us." The grand old \par
document known as the Declaration of Independence trumpeted citizens' rights to \par
"alter" or "abolish" governments in face of a hostile world. Now this. Americans \par
couldn't even get the third world to cough up enough gas to roll their cars out of \par
driveways for traditional summer vacations. Stoned teenagers took over the White \par
House lawn. Freedom seemed a cruel joke or an excuse for chaos. The conservative \par
tabloid, the New York Post, intoned that on "Independence Day, 1979 the American \par
paradox is bleakly apparent. As a nation, we appear to have become steadily more \par
dependent on forces seemingly beyond our control, losing confidence in our ability to \par
master events, uncertain of our direction." And then the editors slipped in this zinger: \par
