FOREIGN POLICY\par
Thank You, Jimmy Carter\par
Restoring the reputation of America\rquote s most underrated foreign\'02policy president.\par
By Justin Va\'efsse, Foreign Policy magazine\par
When the historian Walter Russell Mead in a Foreign Policy article published in \par
early 2010, sought to criticize the Obama administration and warn it against the risks \par
of \ldblquote weakness and indecision\rdblquote  and \ldblquote incoherence and reversals,\rdblquote  the essay\rquote s headline \par
evoked the threat of a \ldblquote Carter syndrome.\rdblquote  The meaning was clear: a damning allusion \par
to President Jimmy Carter\rquote s famously weak foreign-policy record.\par
But there was a problem with Mead\rquote s comparison: The conventional wisdom about \par
Carter is wrong. Far from the feckless leader he\rquote s often portrayed as today, Carter \par
racked up more tangible successes in just four years than most other presidents have \par
in eight.\par
Consider the global situation that Carter bequeathed to Ronald Reagan when he left \par
office in January 1981. Through assertive diplomacy, the outgoing president had \par
dramatically improved America\rquote s global image, then still suffering from the fallout of \par
Vietnam. The Panama Canal treaties \f1\emdash  which Reagan strongly opposed \emdash  had \par
removed an explosive source of irritation from Washington\rquote s relations with Latin \par
America. Carter\rquote s breakthrough at Camp David, where he brokered a peace treaty \par
between Egypt and Israel, had helped stabilize the Middle East. Carter had also \par
managed to establish formal diplomatic relations with Beijing \emdash  an achievement a \par
Republican president would have found difficult to manage, given the sway the \par
Taiwan lobby then held over the party\rquote s ranks.\par
Perhaps the strongest praise for Carter\rquote s legacy came, indirectly, from Reagan \par
himself. Despite having criticized Carter\rquote s late-term policies toward the Soviet Union, \par
not only did Reagan maintain them once taking office \emdash  he expanded them. After the \par
Soviets invaded Afghanistan in 1979, Carter increased the defense budget and \par
embraced a sturdier defense posture, for one, by stationing medium-range nuclear \par
missiles in Europe to match Moscow\rquote s own. Reagan also adopted Carter\rquote s policies of \par
supporting the Afghan mujahideen and imposing sanctions on the trade of advanced \par
technologies with Russia. Like Carter, he also insisted on the implementation of the \par
human rights provisions of the Helsinki Accords, which had been signed by President \par
Gerald Ford in 1975. And Reagan ultimately abided by the terms of the SALT II arms \par
control treaty, though he opposed it during his own presidential bid and it was never \par
ratified by the Senate.\par
Many of Carter\rquote s successes were the result of courageous decisions that required the \par
commitment of immense political capital and proved costly in electoral terms. Nearly \par
one-third of the Senate voted against the ratification of the Panama Canal treaties, \par
129 | P a g e\par
for example. And Carter\rquote s administration wasn\rquote t just brave; it was also unusually \par
scandal-free, particularly when compared with Richard Nixon\rquote s (Watergate) or \par
Reagan\rquote s (the Iran-Contra affair).\par
To be sure, Carter\rquote s record on human rights was decidedly mixed. His administration \par
did reorient U.S. foreign policy by giving unprecedented attention to the issue, \par
promoting the civil rights activist Patricia Derian to the position of assistant \par
secretary of state for human rights and humanitarian affairs, for example, and \par
creating a global issues cluster within the National Security Council, led by National \par
Security Advisor Zbigniew Brzezinski, to address nontraditional security policies. The \par
Carter administration also succeeded in helping to free political prisoners around the \par
world and challenge repression in Argentina, Chile, Ecuador, and Peru.\par
But Carter\rquote s team learned the hard way that it was much easier to push for human \par
rights in small authoritarian countries allied with the United States than in \par
communist states. And his administration was confronted by some hard trade-offs \par
between U.S. interests and moral values, nowhere more so than in Cambodia. At the \par
time, China \emdash  then seen as a useful hedge against the Soviets \emdash  supported the \par
criminal Khmer Rouge regime against Vietnam. To keep Beijing cooperative, Carter \par
ultimately avoided criticizing the murderous behaviour of Pol Pot\rquote s government.\par
Despite Carter\rquote s focus on the failings of U.S. allies, his human rights agenda had a \par
destabilizing impact on his primary adversary, the Soviet leadership. The Kremlin \par
was irritated and dismayed when it realized that Carter was intent on using the \par
Helsinki agreements to empower Soviet dissidents and thus undermine Soviet rule. A \par
recently declassified U.S. intelligence report from 1978 indicates that the Soviets had \par
been hoping for a Ford victory in 1976.\par
They had counted on continuing the direct secret discussions they had held with \par
Nixon and his advisor Henry Kissinger \emdash  discussions that mostly bracketed human \par
rights concerns and that Moscow counted among its greatest postwar successes.\par
Given all this, what explains Carter\rquote s sorry reputation today? The image of weakness \par
was created by a handful of key security decisions made during the first two years of \par
his presidency. These included withdrawing nuclear weapons and some troops from \par
South Korea, deciding to end the B-1 bomber program in June 1977, and stopping the \par
production of the neutron bomb in April 1978. Carter had solid reasons for making \par
each of these choices, but he failed to anticipate the overall effect they would have on \par
his reputation.\par
That\rquote s despite having made many farsighted decisions on defense policy. These \par
included supporting the development of cruise missiles, decreasing U.S. reliance on \par
land-based intercontinental Minuteman missiles (which were relatively vulnerable to \par
Soviet strikes), and modernizing the U.S. Navy\rquote s nuclear-armed Trident submarines. \par
Carter also approved policies that proved critical for U.S. wars in the 1990s and \par
2000s, including financing the next generation of stealth bombers, creating the \par
Carter Doctrine (which guaranteed U.S. military hegemony in the Persian Gulf), and \par
forming a Rapid Deployment Joint Task Force that later became the basis for U.S. \par
Central Command, the Defense Department\rquote s unified command for the Middle East.\par
130 | P a g e\par
Of course, the Carter administration did suffer several unmistakable failures. One \par
was the overthrow of Shah Mohammed Reza Pahlavi\rquote s authoritarian but pro\'02American government by Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini\rquote s Islamist and violently anti\'02Western regime in Iran. Then came the interminable humiliation of the Iran hostage \par
crisis, when U.S. diplomats and citizens were held captive for 444 days. In April \par
1980, the disastrous and deadly end of Operation Eagle Claw, a military raid \par
designed to free the hostages, increased the perception of America\rquote s \emdash  and Carter\rquote s \par
own \emdash  weakness. (Though it was Carter, not Reagan, who ultimately freed the \par
hostages through diplomatic means.) And then, of course, came Carter\rquote s electoral \par
defeat in November 1980, which cast a pall of failure over his entire balance sheet.\par
The other general explanation for Carter\rquote s poor reputation today is his persistent \par
inability to explain and promote his administration\rquote s decisions and achievements. \par
The president and his team set maximally ambitious goals, such as eliminating \par
nuclear weapons. Although they met a good number of them, they didn\rquote t achieve\par
them all \emdash  or at least not as fully as they had promised. The result, inevitably, was \par
disappointment. The rhetoric of human rights, even without hyperbole, contributed \par
to the disillusionment when inevitable compromises had to be made between values \par
and national security. The same phenomenon of backing down from overly \par
optimistic declarations was evident in the administration\rquote s reaction to the Soviet \par
invasion of Afghanistan (described by Carter as \ldblquote the most serious threat to world \par
peace since the Second World War\rdblquote  but followed by largely symbolic retaliatory \par
measures, such as boycotting the Olympic Games). And by promising to concentrate \par
on freeing the hostages in Iran rather than campaigning for re-election (the Rose \par
Garden strategy), Carter allowed the crisis to hold him hostage as well \emdash  only \par
highlighting his apparent helplessness.\par
But these failures can ultimately be attributed to a poor communications strategy \par
and a troubled geopolitical environment. The substance of the Carter \par
administration\rquote s foreign policy, by contrast, should be celebrated. And comparisons \par
to his legacy should be anything but an insult.\par
